\f1\b\fs36 \cf2 \strokec2 Appendix: Future Anomalies & Black Swan Considerations\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa160\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf2 The "Black Swan" Limitation\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa200\partightenfactor0

\f0\b0\fs24 \cf2 The 5, 10, and 20-year projections in this report rely on the 
\f1\b Stationarity Assumption
\f0\b0 \'97the idea that the underlying mechanisms of the economy will remain constant. However, as demonstrated by the 2010 FASB change and the 2020 COVID-19 pandemic, consumer debt is highly susceptible to "Black Swan" events: unpredictable occurrences that deviate far beyond what past data would suggest.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa160\partightenfactor0

\f1\b\fs28 \cf2 Potential Factors for Future Deviations\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa200\partightenfactor0

\f0\b0\fs24 \cf2 To provide a more robust outlook, the following factors should be considered as potential causes for the model's failure to maintain a linear path:\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa160\partightenfactor0

\f1\b \cf2 1. Legislative & Regulatory "Jubilees"\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa200\partightenfactor0

\f0\b0 \cf2 A massive federal intervention, such as widespread credit card debt forgiveness or a student loan jubilee, would cause an immediate, vertical downward shift in the debt trend. This would be a 
\f1\b Structural Break
\f0\b0  similar to the 2010 accounting spike, but in the opposite direction.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa160\partightenfactor0

\f1\b \cf2 2. The "Hidden Debt" of FinTech (BNPL)\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa200\partightenfactor0

\f0\b0 \cf2 Traditional "Revolving Credit" data (FRED) may begin to lag behind reality due to the rise of 
\f1\b Buy-Now-Pay-Later (BNPL)
\f0\b0  services. Since many of these loans are not currently reported as revolving credit, the actual consumer debt burden might be significantly higher than the linear model suggests, leading to an "under-estimation" bias.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa160\partightenfactor0

\f1\b \cf2 3. Hyper-Inflationary Detachment\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa200\partightenfactor0

\f0\b0 \cf2 If the Consumer Price Index (CPI) enters a non-linear, exponential growth phase, consumer debt may "detach" from its historical linear slope. In this scenario, debt would likely follow a 
\f1\b Parabolic Curve
\f0\b0  as consumers use credit to cover the exponentially rising costs of survival (food, energy, housing).\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa160\partightenfactor0

\f1\b \cf2 4. Digital Currency Shifts (CBDCs)\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa200\partightenfactor0

\f0\b0 \cf2 The potential introduction of a Central Bank Digital Currency could fundamentally change how credit is issued or tracked. This would likely result in another 
\f1\b Data Reclassification event
\f0\b0  (similar to FASB 2010), rendering historical data incomparable to future metrics.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
